A||||16||Steven||3.2 conversion is complete!!!||||4||
Z||000000||Steven||06-18-2001||02:25 PM||||Well Saturday, I finished hooking everything up to the engine, primed the motor, and started it for the first time! It sounded great! Ran it for 20 mins to break the cam in. After that I took it for a little test ride, and ran in to one small problem. The "check engine" light came on. Coded that and found a map sensor code. Looked at the map sensor, vac. hose was on, pushed the connector up, and the lock tab clicked. Looked like it was on, but wasn't.Cleared the codes, and started it up again. Took it for another little test ride. Runs strong, will let you know more about it once the engine has had time to break in. ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Wade87GT||06-18-2001||04:16 PM||||Yes, please keep us posted on your progress (me anyway). I've become a bit worn out on piecing together "bolt-on" upgrades and others. Even though my 155,000 mile 2.8 is running the strongest ever and doesn't use any oil, I want to be ready for the rebuild.<P>Wade||||reg||14
Z||000002||Cplensdorf||06-18-2001||04:20 PM||||What process did you use to pre-oil your engine before start-up.  I have got the jitters about cranking my new 3.2L over for the first time. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||06-18-2001||04:36 PM||||Where are the pictures Steve? You beat me to it. Glade yours went smoothly. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steven||06-18-2001||04:44 PM||||The way I did it I am sure some people will say is wrong or that their way is better. I had primed the oil pump by hand prior to installing it in the engine. After putting everything as far back togather as I could with the engine on the engine stand, I hooked up a pressure gage to the block, and used a drill motor with a socket to turn the oil pump drive shaft. Had good oil pressure. Put the engine in the cradle, and lifter the cradle into the car. When it came time to start it, I unpluged the coil, and cranked it over I think 3 times for about 15 seconds each time till the oil pressure gage showed pressure. Now some will say that is wrong because you are starting it dry. But my thinking was no your not. Remember all of the heavy oil (cam lube/engine lube/GM's EOS) and the moly lube that you put on the moving parts is still there doing it job of proteching the parts. It is going to take more time to push that stuff from between the bearings and the rotating parts then just the few times it cranks over. After I had pressure, I plugged in the coil, and started it. Ran it for 20 mins to break in the cam. Everything is fine! Some people will tell you to use the drill just prior to start up. You do what you want. <P>------------------<BR>85 Fiero GT<BR>3.2 liter w/4sp manual trans<BR>Silver.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Greg Piet||06-18-2001||04:44 PM||||Uhhh  as far as pre lubing a newly put together motor, I am hoping you used engine assembly lube when installing the parts!<P>I used A LOT of clevite high pressure assembly lube(red).<P>If you did not do that, I reccomend getting a longer hex rod that fits into the oil pump and sticks out beyond the distributor mounting hole and use a drill to run the oil pump before starting the car (the distributor needs to be  off to do this obviously).<P>That or take apart the motor and put it together with assembly lube.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Steven||06-18-2001||04:48 PM||||88,<BR>Yeah, but your motor will still be bigger. Pics will fallow shortly. How far along are you on yours?<P>Greg, <BR>I used lot assembly lube. I used GM's EOS (Engine Oil Supplement) prelube. Part number 1052367. <p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 06-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||06-18-2001||06:34 PM||||I'm stuck!!! I ran out of money. I can't find half of my valve cover bolts and I think I have the wrong oil pump. My FOCOA headers haven’t come in yet.  I can't find the little threaded brass coupling for the oil filter. My Main studs arn't in yet. <P>This is what happens when you start to put the motor together a year later.<P>Sounds to me like you did a good job priming the motor. You should change the oil after the break in though just in case the assembly lube starts to clog the oil filter. Just a precaution. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Leadfoot||06-18-2001||06:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wade87GT:<BR><B>Yes, please keep us posted on your progress (me anyway). I've become a bit worn out on piecing together "bolt-on" upgrades and others. Even though my 155,000 mile 2.8 is running the strongest ever and doesn't use any oil, I want to be ready for the rebuild.<P>Wade</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>You might want to replace your rod bearings about now.  My 85SE had 157k on it when they went.  Then again, you probably don't redline yours 99% of the time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Steven||06-18-2001||11:00 PM||||88, <BR>I might have 6- 8 valve cover bolts, let me check and if I find them you can have them. <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jam021.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jam022.jpg">  <BR>Now I still have some things to do. Like by the "Fiero GT" sticker for the plenum from Rodney Dickman, buy my red ign. wires, the MSD coil, and of course clean the top half of the engine bay. I did clean a lot of the areas that I knew I would not be able to get to once the motor was in. <p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
